# Local settings of the Worker for `pnpm dev` and `pnpm preview`. Optional:
# without a .dev.vars file, sign-in is simulated and the admin is
# admin@example.com. To change that, copy this file to .dev.vars, which git
# ignores. `pnpm e2e` and `pnpm preview:e2e` never read it.

# Who can always sign in, as an admin. Comma-separated, each entry a bare
# email address. Any other entry is ignored, with an error in the log.
ADMIN_EMAILS=admin@example.com

# The OAuth client of a Google Cloud project. With both set, sign-in goes to
# the real Google instead of the simulated one. The client needs the redirect
# address http://localhost:5173/api/auth/google/callback, and for
# `pnpm preview` the same on port 4173.
#GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=
#GOOGLE_CLIENT_SECRET=

# The keys that the credentials of connected accounts are sealed with before
# they are stored. Without this line, `pnpm dev` uses a key from the
# repository, which is fine for trying things out and protects nothing.
# `pnpm preview` does not: it serves a production build, which has no such
# key, and without this line it refuses to connect accounts, with a 503. A
# comma-separated list of "<version>:<key>", each key 32 random bytes in
# base64: make one with `openssl rand -base64 32`. The highest version
# seals. To replace a key, add one with a higher version and keep the old
# one, which still opens what it sealed. Whatever was stored under other
# keys can no longer be read: connect those accounts again.
#CREDENTIAL_KEYS=1:

# The keys that pigy signs with as a client of Bluesky. Bluesky reads pigy's
# description, with the public halves of these keys, from
# <address>/connect/bluesky/client-metadata.json. Without this line,
# `pnpm dev` uses a key from the repository where it is reached at
# localhost, which is enough for the sandbox. A real Bluesky account cannot
# be connected from localhost at all: Bluesky's servers cannot read pigy's
# description there. `pnpm preview` has no such key, and without this line
# it cannot connect Bluesky accounts, in the sandbox either. A
# comma-separated list of "<version>:<key>", each key a private key of P-256
# in PKCS #8 and base64: make one with
#   openssl genpkey -algorithm EC -pkeyopt ec_paramgen_curve:P-256 | openssl pkcs8 -topk8 -nocrypt -outform DER | openssl base64 -A
# The highest version signs new connections. A connection stays bound to the
# key that it began with: keep a key for as long as such a connection is to
# last, or connect those accounts again.
#BLUESKY_CLIENT_KEYS=1:

# The application that you have made for this pigy in LinkedIn's developer
# portal, https://www.linkedin.com/developers/apps, with the products "Share
# on LinkedIn" and "Sign In with LinkedIn using OpenID Connect", and with
# the redirect address <address>/connect/linkedin/callback: for `pnpm dev`
# http://localhost:5173/connect/linkedin/callback. Without these two lines,
# no LinkedIn account can be connected in a live workspace. The sandbox
# needs neither: its simulated LinkedIn has an application of its own.
#LINKEDIN_CLIENT_ID=
#LINKEDIN_CLIENT_SECRET=

# The app that you have made for this pigy in Meta's dashboard for
# developers, https://developers.facebook.com/apps, with the use case
# "Access the Threads API", the permissions threads_basic,
# threads_content_publish, threads_manage_replies, threads_read_replies and
# threads_delete, and the redirect callback address
# <address>/connect/threads/callback. Meta takes only HTTPS addresses, so a
# pigy at http://localhost connects no real Threads account. The two values
# are the Threads app ID and the Threads app secret of the use case, not
# those of the Meta app. Until Meta has reviewed the app, only the accounts
# that you add as Threads testers can be connected. Without these two
# lines, no Threads account can be connected in a live workspace. The
# sandbox needs neither: its simulated Threads has an app of its own.
#THREADS_APP_ID=
#THREADS_APP_SECRET=
# The key that pigy signs push messages with, to the devices that have
# turned notifications on. A browser subscribes with its public half.
# Without this line, `pnpm dev` uses a key from the repository where it is
# reached at localhost, and has no notifications all the same: it has no
# service worker. `pnpm preview` has one and no such key, and without this
# line Settings say there that notifications are not set up. A private key
# of P-256 in PKCS #8 and base64, by itself: make one with
#   openssl genpkey -algorithm EC -pkeyopt ec_paramgen_curve:P-256 | openssl pkcs8 -topk8 -nocrypt -outform DER | openssl base64 -A
# Replacing it turns the notifications of every device off: each has to
# turn them on again.
#VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=
# The address that this pigy is reached at from the internet, without a
# path. Threads fetches the images of a post from pigy, at a link that pigy
# makes with this address: without this line no post with an image goes to
# a real Threads account. Which a pigy at localhost cannot do anyway:
# Threads cannot reach it. The sandbox needs none.
#APP_URL=
